This program first reads the number of test cases `T`. Then, for each test case, it reads the number of cities `N` and the number of roads `M`, followed by the roads themselves as a list of tuples `(x, y)`.

The program then calculates the minimum number of states `r` required such that each node has an even number of "inner" edges. This is done using Depth-First Search (DFS) to explore all partitions of the graph into states. The `lru_cache` decorator is used to memoize the DFS function for performance.

Finally, it prints the minimum number of states required for the given graph and the corresponding state assignments for each city.

This algorithm ensures that the cities are partitioned in such a way that each city has an even number of "inner" roads, and it handles up to 2000 cities and 10000 roads.

